  "query_preview": "..Today's chances for showers and thunderstorms have\ntrended later given a delayed push of tropical moisture aloft,\nwith only some isolated showers and thunderstorms possible across\nthe higher terrain through the early afternoon. Activity becomes\nmore scattered this evening, with coverage favored first across\nsouthwest Utah before spreading northeastward through early Monday\nmorning. Given the environment is not yet primed with deep\nmoisture, main concerns for storms through this period will be \ngusty outflow winds to 50 mph, particularly across the West Desert\nand northern Utah where the lower levels remain sufficiently dry.\n\nAfter a lull in activity across southern Utah overnight behind the\nfirst push of convection, widespread rainfall pushes into southern\nUtah early Monday morning as an anomalous upper-level low begins\nto track into the Desert Southwest. This low will tap into\nsubstantial amounts of tropical moisture originating from\nHurricanes Polo and Odalys in the Eastern Pacific, while \nconcurrently producing ample instability given strong diffluence\naloft. As such, showers and thunderstorms overspreading the\nregion, especially across southern and central Utah where PWAT\nanomalies are highest, will be very efficient rainmakers. Some \nrecent CAMs runs suggest the potential for stronger cells to \nproduce rainfall rates of 0.7-1.0\" per hour at times between \nMonday morning and Monday evening, particularly across the \nsouthern Utah mountains, Zion National Park area, and the San \nRafael Swell. \n\nFlash flooding will be the main threat between Monday morning and\nTuesday morning, especially across portions of central and \nsouthern Utah generally east of the I-15 corridor where \ninstability and moisture is maximized; a Flood Watch has been \nissued for these areas given the heightened risk of widespread \nflash flooding. One limiting factor to the flash flood risk is the\npotential for precipitation to become more stratiform, especially\nacross southeast Utah, as cloud cover overspreading the region \nahead of the main shield of precipitation Monday morning may limit\ninstability in this area. Regardless, those heading outdoors \nacross these areas should consider alternate plans and those \nliving in these areas should be alert should any Flash Flood \nWarnings be issued. \n\nDrier air quickly moves in from the northeast early Tuesday as the\nlow continues to drop southeastward into Arizona, allowing showers\nto taper off across most of northern and central Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming through the morning hours. Moisture wrapping \naround the northern periphery of the low will linger across \nsoutheast Utah throughout the day, however, keeping activity going\nin this area. Given soils will be primed from recent heavy \nrainfall, the flash flood risk will remain heightened especially\nTuesday afternoon before dry northerly flow overtakes the \nremainder of the region overnight into Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure develops across the western US on Wednesday and\nremains the dominant feature overhead through the remainder of the\nweek. Models were previously hinting at the potential for a\nshortwave to impact the region on Saturday, but has trended\nfurther north with little impact to our region other than\nmoderating temperatures.",
